# The Zig toolchain, installed from the upstream tarball into /opt.
#
# That tarball puts the compiler at the TOP of its directory - there is no bin/
# subdirectory - so no base-policy rule reaches it and it keeps usr_t, which is not
# an exec type. Nothing complains until something probes executability: eselect's zig
# module builds its list with [[ -x /usr/bin/zig-bin-* ]], follows the symlink to this
# binary, and gets EACCES, so `sudo eselect zig list` printed "(none found)" and `set`
# rejected the target. The same command as the user worked - the difference was the
# domain (staff_t has usr_t execute somewhere in its inherited grants, sysadm_t does
# not), not the file's permissions (measured 2026-10-11).
#
# bin_t is the honest label for a compiler binary (gcc is bin_t), and it is what lets
# every domain that may execute ordinary binaries see it as executable: staff_t,
# sysadm_t, and the portage domains that an ebuild shelling out to zig would use.
/opt/zig-bin-[^/]*/zig	--	gen_context(system_u:object_r:bin_t,s0)
# The AMD KFD (compute) node. Base policy has no rule for it, so it keeps device_t - a type granted to
# almost nobody on purpose - and every open fails EACCES with nothing in the audit (device_t accesses are
# dontaudited). The render nodes beside it get dri_device_t from /dev/dri/.+, which is why Vulkan works
# and ROCm cannot see the card. Measured 2026-10-24.
/dev/kfd	-c	gen_context(system_u:object_r:kfd_device_t,s0)
